<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecclesia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ekklesia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ecclesia, or ekklesia, was the assembly of the citizens in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, best known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where it was open to all male citizens; in 594 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> allowed all Athenian citizens to take part regardless of class. The Athenian assembly declared war, elected the strategoi and other officials, had the final say on legislation and called magistrates to account after their year of office. Its agenda was set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">Boule</a>, and it voted by show of hands, counting of stones and broken pottery. A typical meeting probably held around 6,000 of 30,000–60,000 citizens; it met on the Pnyx, and around 300 BCE its meetings moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Citizens were registered in about 139 local demes, grouped into ten new tribes that cut across regional loyalties, and each tribe supplied fifty members, chosen by lot, to the Council of 500, which prepared business for the Assembly, or <b>ekklesia</b>, of all adult male citizens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Athens the Assembly (<b>ekklesia</b>), open to all adult male citizens, met on the hill of the Pnyx about forty times a year in the 4th century BCE and decided by show of hands on war and peace, treaties, finance and honours.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
